---
import BlogPostLayout from '../../../../layouts/BlogPostLayout.astro';

const title = 'Digital Marketing Career Path India: The Real Role Ladder, Executive to CMO';
const description =
  'The digital marketing career path in India, role by role: executive, specialist, manager, and head of marketing/CMO, with real salary jumps and how to pick your specialization.';
const canonical =
  'https://futurecareerschool.com/blog/career-options/digital-marketing-career-path-india-role-progression';
const publishedDateISO = '2026-07-21';
const publishedDateLabel = '21 July 2026';
const authorName = 'Shivanshi Sehgal';
const authorUrl = 'https://futurecareerschool.com/about/shivanshi-sehgal/';
const authorRole = 'Career researcher and writer';

const jumpLinks = [
  { id: 'short-answer', label: 'The short answer' },
  { id: 'the-ladder', label: 'The four-rung ladder' },
  { id: 'rung-executive', label: 'Rung 1: Executive' },
  { id: 'rung-specialist', label: 'Rung 2: Specialist' },
  { id: 'rung-manager', label: 'Rung 3: Manager' },
  { id: 'rung-head', label: 'Rung 4: Head of Marketing / CMO' },
  { id: 'forks', label: 'The specialization forks' },
  { id: 'pick-a-fork', label: 'How to pick your fork' },
  { id: 'agency-vs-inhouse', label: 'Agency vs in-house pace' },
  { id: 'proof-and-skills', label: 'Certifications and proof that matter' },
  { id: 'mistakes', label: 'What stalls this ladder' },
  { id: 'related-reads', label: 'If you have a different question' },
  { id: 'next-step', label: 'What to do next' },
  { id: 'faq', label: 'FAQs' },
];

const ladderRungs = [
  {
    id: 'rung-executive',
    num: '01',
    title: 'Digital Marketing Executive (roughly 0-2 years)',
    body: 'You run campaigns someone else designed: scheduling posts, setting up basic ad campaigns, writing on-page SEO tags, pulling reports. The job is breadth, not depth. Entry-level executive pay in India typically sits around ₹2.5-6 LPA, with the wide spread coming from city, company size, and whether you already have a certification or internship proof behind you.',
    tip: 'The real task at this rung is not "do the job," it is "find out which channel you actually enjoy and are good at." Most people who stall later never did this honestly at rung one.',
  },
  {
    id: 'rung-specialist',
    num: '02',
    title: 'Digital Marketing Specialist / Senior Executive (roughly 2-4 years)',
    body: 'You now own one channel end to end: SEO, paid media, content, email/CRM, or social. Titles here vary a lot by company (SEO Specialist, Performance Marketing Associate, Content Strategist), but the underlying shift is the same: you are trusted with a budget or a metric, not just a task list. Pay typically moves to roughly ₹6-14 LPA depending on the channel and how measurable your results are.',
    tip: 'Paid channels (search ads, performance marketing) usually show salary movement faster here because results are directly attributable to spend. SEO and content specialists often catch up by year 4-5 once they have a portfolio of ranked pages or a body of work that moved a real number.',
  },
  {
    id: 'rung-manager',
    num: '03',
    title: 'Digital Marketing Manager (roughly 4-8 years)',
    body: 'You now own a channel mix or an entire brand/business unit\'s digital presence, with a small team or a set of vendors reporting through you. The job shifts from executing to planning: budget allocation across channels, hiring and reviewing specialists, and defending a marketing plan to a business head. AmbitionBox and Payscale data put manager-level pay in the roughly ₹8-20 LPA band for 4-7 years of experience, with mid-career (3-7 years) ranges reported as wide as ₹12-35 LPA once bonuses, company type, and city are factored in.',
    tip: 'Software product companies tend to pay meaningfully more than traditional marketing and advertising agencies at this exact rung, sometimes ₹4-5 LPA higher for a similar title, because product companies treat marketing as a revenue lever, not a cost center.',
  },
  {
    id: 'rung-head',
    num: '04',
    title: 'Head of Marketing / VP Marketing / CMO (roughly 8-15+ years)',
    body: 'This is where the job stops being channel work entirely. A Head of Digital or Senior Manager (roughly 8-12 years) owns cross-channel strategy and a bigger team. A VP of Marketing or CMO (typically 12-20+ years) owns P&L, brand positioning, and the marketing function\'s seat at the leadership table. Senior manager-level digital marketing pay in India runs roughly ₹35-90 LPA at 8+ years, and Director/VP/CMO compensation stretches from roughly ₹50 LPA past ₹1 crore, heavily dependent on company size, industry, and whether equity is part of the package.',
    tip: 'Very few people reach CMO through digital marketing execution alone. The ones who do usually added one thing along the way: a track record of connecting marketing spend to revenue, not just to engagement metrics.',
  },
];

const forkCards = [
  {
    title: 'SEO and organic growth',
    fit: 'You enjoy research, pattern-spotting, and playing a long game where results compound over months, not days.',
    watch: 'Slower to show results early, which can feel discouraging if you need quick wins to build confidence or a portfolio.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Paid media / performance marketing',
    fit: 'You like numbers, fast feedback loops, and testing variables where you see a result within days, not months.',
    watch: 'Constant platform changes (ad policy, auction dynamics, tracking/privacy shifts) mean you are always relearning the tools, not just the strategy.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Content and brand storytelling',
    fit: 'You are a strong writer or communicator who thinks in narratives and enjoys shaping how an audience perceives a company.',
    watch: 'Harder to prove direct ROI early in your career, so build proof through engagement, lead-gen, or SEO traffic tied to your content, not vague "reach" numbers.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Marketing analytics',
    fit: 'You like being the person who explains why a campaign worked or failed, using data instead of opinion, and you are comfortable in spreadsheets or a BI tool.',
    watch: 'This fork rewards genuine statistical literacy. Weak data instincts get exposed fast once you are asked to defend a number in a leadership meeting.',
  },
  {
    title: 'Brand and growth strategy',
    fit: 'You think in systems: how positioning, pricing, channel mix, and retention all connect to one growth number, not just one campaign.',
    watch: 'This is usually not an entry fork. Most people move here after 4-6 years of proving themselves in one or two of the other forks first.',
  },
];

const pickForkRows = [
  { area: 'Ideal starting temperament', seo: 'Patient, research-driven', paid: 'Fast-paced, comfortable with numbers', content: 'Strong writer, audience-minded', analytics: 'Detail-oriented, comfortable with data tools' },
  { area: 'Time to see your own impact', seo: 'Months', paid: 'Days to weeks', content: 'Weeks to months', analytics: 'Immediate, but indirect' },
  { area: 'Freelance/consulting demand', seo: 'High, especially local SEO', paid: 'High, but client trust takes longer to build', content: 'Moderate to high', analytics: 'Growing, often bundled into a broader role' },
  { area: 'Typical early salary edge', seo: 'Slower start, catches up by year 4-5', paid: 'Faster early salary growth', content: 'Similar to SEO, slower start', analytics: 'Strong if paired with a channel skill' },
];

const mistakeItems = [
  {
    num: '01',
    title: 'Staying a generalist past year 3',
    body: 'Being able to touch every channel a little is useful at rung one. Past that, "I can do a bit of everything" reads as "I am not deep in anything" to a hiring manager comparing you against someone with two years of focused, measurable SEO or paid media proof.',
  },
  {
    num: '02',
    title: 'Chasing a manager title without team or budget proof',
    body: 'A title jump from a small company that never actually gave you ownership of a budget or a person does not transfer well when you interview at a company that takes the manager title literally. Interviewers ask specific questions about budget size, team size, and what you personally decided, not what you executed.',
  },
  {
    num: '03',
    title: 'Ignoring analytics because "that is someone else\'s job"',
    body: 'Every fork above eventually needs you to defend a number to a non-marketing stakeholder. SEO specialists who cannot explain organic traffic attribution, or content marketers who cannot connect a piece to a lead, hit a ceiling earlier than peers who built even basic analytics literacy alongside their core skill.',
  },
  {
    num: '04',
    title: 'Job-hopping for title inflation instead of scope',
    body: 'Moving companies every year for a bigger title but the same actual scope of work (same budget size, same team size, same decisions) creates a resume that looks senior on paper and thin in the interview room. Scope should grow with the title, not just the label.',
  },
];

const faqItems = [
  {
    q: 'What is the real digital marketing career path in India, role by role?',
    a: 'Four practical rungs: Digital Marketing Executive (roughly 0-2 years, learning and executing across channels), Specialist or Senior Executive (roughly 2-4 years, owning one channel), Manager (roughly 4-8 years, owning a channel mix or a brand\'s digital presence with a team or vendors under you), and Head of Marketing, VP, or CMO (roughly 8-15+ years, owning strategy, P&L, and the leadership seat). Titles vary by company, but the shift in responsibility at each rung is consistent.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Which digital marketing specialization should I pick: SEO, paid media, content, or analytics?',
    a: 'Match it to your working style, not to whichever pays most this year. SEO and content suit people who are patient and enjoy research or storytelling with results that compound over months. Paid media and analytics suit people who like fast feedback loops and numbers. Paid channels often show faster early salary growth because results are directly attributable to spend, but experienced SEO and content specialists typically catch up by year four or five once they have a real portfolio of ranked work or attributed leads.',
  },
  {
    q: 'How long does it take to reach digital marketing manager level in India?',
    a: 'Most people reach manager-level responsibility, owning a budget and a small team or set of vendors, somewhere around four to eight years, depending on how early they specialized and how measurable their results were. Staying a generalist well past year three tends to slow this down, since manager roles are usually filled by people who can point to deep ownership of one channel or function first.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Is freelancing a faster route to higher digital marketing income than the employed career ladder?',
    a: 'It can be, but it is a genuinely different track with different risks: inconsistent income, self-managed client acquisition, and no built-in team or budget to grow into. If the question you actually have is whether to freelance instead of climbing the employed ladder, the dedicated guide on digital marketing freelancing in India how to start covers the sub-skill packaging, first-client, and pricing decisions that this article does not.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Can I switch into digital marketing from a non-marketing background or after a career break?',
    a: 'Yes, and it is one of the more switch-friendly careers because rung one rewards demonstrated skill over a specific degree. The fastest route in is usually a free or low-cost certification paired with one self-built project, a small business audit, a personal blog you ranked, or a mock ad campaign, that gives you something concrete to show at interview instead of only a certificate. Career changers and people returning after a break typically enter at the executive rung regardless of prior seniority in another field, since the proof, not the resume gap or the past title, is what gets weighed.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Does AI change where this career ladder is heading?',
    a: 'Yes, meaningfully, at the execution layer. Routine tasks like ad copy variations, basic reporting, and first-draft content are increasingly AI-assisted, which raises the bar at the executive and specialist rungs specifically. The roles that hold up best are the ones built on judgment, strategy, and stakeholder communication, which is exactly what the manager and head-of-marketing rungs already demand. The fuller breakdown of what AI is automating and which skills now pay more lives in how AI is changing digital marketing careers in India.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Do I need certifications to move up the digital marketing ladder in India?',
    a: 'Not on their own. Free or low-cost certifications (Google Skillshop, HubSpot Academy, Meta Blueprint) are enough to cover the fundamentals for most forks, and hiring managers weigh a documented project with a real, measurable result far more heavily than a certificate with no story behind it. Spend on a paid certification or bootcamp only when it adds mentorship, a real project with feedback, or placement access you genuinely cannot get for free.',
  },
  {
    q: 'Should I choose digital marketing or data analytics as a career instead?',
    a: 'They overlap more than people expect, especially in the analytics fork of digital marketing, but they are genuinely different day-to-day jobs with different entry barriers and different personality fits. If you are deciding between the two fields entirely, rather than picking a specialization within digital marketing, the side-by-side comparison in digital marketing vs data analytics career India covers daily work, entry barrier, salary, and growth ceiling for both.',
  },
];

const jsonLd = [
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
    itemListElement: [
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://futurecareerschool.com/' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Blog', item: 'https://futurecareerschool.com/blog' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Career Options', item: 'https://futurecareerschool.com/blog/career-options' },
      { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 4, name: 'Digital Marketing Career Path India', item: canonical },
    ],
  },
  {
    '@context': 'https://schema.org',
    '@type': 'FAQPage',
    mainEntity: faqItems.map((item) => ({
      '@type': 'Question',
      name: item.q,
      acceptedAnswer: { '@type': 'Answer', text: item.a },
    })),
  },
];
---

<BlogPostLayout
  title={title}
  description={description}
  canonical={canonical}
  category="career-options"
  publishedAt={publishedDateISO}
  publishedLabel={publishedDateLabel}
  authorName={authorName}
  authorUrl={authorUrl}
  authorRole={authorRole}
  jsonLdExtras={jsonLd}
>
  <p class="lead-para">
    The <strong>digital marketing career path in India</strong> for anyone taking the employed or agency
    route runs through four real rungs: executive, specialist, manager, and head of marketing or CMO. Each
    rung asks for a different kind of proof, not just more years, and the specialization you pick along the
    way, SEO, paid media, content, analytics, or brand and growth, decides how fast you move through them
    and how early you reach real financial freedom instead of a plateaued salary.
  </p>
  <p>
    If you are building a career inside a company or agency, with a boss, a team, and a budget that is not
    entirely your own, this ladder is the one you are actually climbing. If your real question is narrower,
    three related answers sit further down this page: whether to skip the ladder and freelance instead,
    whether digital marketing or data analytics fits you better as a field, or what AI is actually changing
    about this work.
  </p>

  <div class="key-takeaways">
    <p class="key-takeaways__label">The short version</p>
    <ul>
      <li>Four rungs: Executive (0-2 yrs, ₹2.5-6 LPA) → Specialist (2-4 yrs, ₹6-14 LPA) → Manager (4-8 yrs, ₹8-20+ LPA) → Head of Marketing/CMO (8-15+ yrs, ₹35 LPA to ₹1 Cr+).</li>
      <li>Pick a specialization by year 2-3: SEO, paid media, content, analytics, or brand/growth. Match it to your working style, not just the fastest-paying option.</li>
      <li>Paid media and analytics tend to show salary movement faster early on; SEO and content specialists typically catch up by year 4-5 with a real portfolio.</li>
      <li>The single biggest thing that stalls this ladder is staying a generalist past year three instead of going deep in one channel first.</li>
      <li>Climbing this ladder deliberately, deep channel skill plus visible proof, is what turns digital marketing into a genuine high-income skill portfolio instead of a job that plateaus at mid-level pay.</li>
      <li>Add AI workflow skill, business and customer judgment, communication, analytics, and personal market proof. That stack raises the ceiling through strategy leadership, consulting, an agency, content assets, or ownership—not simply managing more campaigns.</li>
    </ul>
  </div>

  <div class="cta-inline">
    <a class="btn-gold pulse" href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">Get guidance on your next rung</a>
    <a class="btn-outline cta-assessment-lite" href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/assessments">Use a free assessment as a quick first check</a>
  </div>

  <p>
    This ladder sits inside our wider <a href="/blog/career-options/">career options</a> guides, which walk
    through what a role actually involves, what to learn, and how to start, one decision at a time.
  </p>

  <nav class="jump-nav" aria-label="Jump to section">
    <span class="jump-nav__label">Jump to section</span>
    <ul class="jump-nav__list">
      {jumpLinks.map((link) => (
        <li><a href={`#${link.id}`}>{link.label}</a></li>
      ))}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 id="short-answer">The short answer</h2>
  <p>
    Digital marketing role progression in India follows a fairly consistent shape once you strip away the
    title variation between companies: you start by executing across channels, you specialize in one channel
    within two to three years, you start owning budget and people once you are trusted with outcomes, and
    you eventually move from managing channels to managing the whole marketing function's strategy and P&L.
    The pace through these rungs depends far more on how early and how deeply you specialize than on how
    many years you have logged.
  </p>

  <h2 id="the-ladder">The four-rung ladder</h2>
  <p>
    Company titles are messy. A "Digital Marketing Manager" at a 20-person startup and a "Digital Marketing
    Manager" at a large FMCG brand can mean two very different jobs. What stays consistent across companies
    is the shift in what you are actually trusted to decide, so that is what each rung below is built around.
  </p>

  <h2 id="rung-detail">The rungs in detail</h2>
  <div class="protocol-stack">
    {ladderRungs.map((item) => (
      <div class="protocol-row" id={item.id}>
        <span class="protocol-num">{item.num}</span>
        <div class="protocol-body">
          <h3>{item.title}</h3>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
          <p class="protocol-note">{item.tip}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>
  <div class="honest-take">
    <p class="honest-take__label">Honest take</p>
    <p>
      Salary numbers reported for "digital marketing manager" in India span a genuinely wide band, roughly
      ₹8 LPA to ₹35 LPA depending on the source, city, and company type, because the title itself is used
      loosely across a tiny 15-person agency and a 500-person product company. Treat any single number you
      see quoted online as a starting point for research, not a promise. What consistently moves the number
      up within that band is measurable ownership: a budget size, a team size, or a metric you can defend in
      a room.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="forks">The specialization forks</h2>
  <p>
    Somewhere between rung one and rung two, most people in digital marketing pick a lane, whether they
    decide it deliberately or drift into it by default. Deciding deliberately is what shortens the path to
    manager-level trust.
  </p>
  <div class="track-grid">
    {forkCards.map((card) => (
      <div class="track-card">
        <h3>{card.title}</h3>
        <div class="track-subline track-subline--fit"><strong>Fit:</strong> {card.fit}</div>
        <div class="track-subline track-subline--watch"><strong>Watch out:</strong> {card.watch}</div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="pick-a-fork">How to pick your fork, based on aptitude</h2>
  <p>
    Do not pick a specialization purely off a salary screenshot someone posted online. Match it to how you
    actually like to work, because the specialists who stay in a fork long enough to get genuinely good at
    it are the ones who reach manager level fastest, not the ones who switch channels every year chasing
    whichever one looks hottest. <strong>The fork you choose is really a decision about which high-income
    skill you are building for the next decade</strong>, so weigh it against your temperament and financial
    goals, not just this year's hiring trend.
  </p>
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table>
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Area</th>
          <th>SEO</th>
          <th>Paid media</th>
          <th>Content</th>
          <th>Analytics</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        {pickForkRows.map((row) => (
          <tr>
            <td data-label="Area">{row.area}</td>
            <td data-label="SEO">{row.seo}</td>
            <td data-label="Paid media">{row.paid}</td>
            <td data-label="Content">{row.content}</td>
            <td data-label="Analytics">{row.analytics}</td>
          </tr>
        ))}
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <p>
    Brand and growth strategy is left out of the table above deliberately: it is rarely a starting fork.
    Most people who end up in brand or growth roles got there after four to six years proving themselves
    inside one of the four channels above first, then adding the cross-channel thinking on top.
  </p>

  <h2 id="agency-vs-inhouse">Agency vs in-house: the pace difference</h2>
  <p>
    Agencies tend to move you through rungs one and two faster because you are exposed to many clients and
    channels at once, which builds breadth quickly. The trade-off is that agency roles often cap out earlier
    on pay at the specialist and manager rungs compared to in-house roles at larger companies, particularly
    software product companies, which typically pay meaningfully more for the same manager-level title
    because they treat marketing as a direct revenue lever rather than a service cost.
  </p>
  <div class="callout callout--gold">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">Practical read</p>
    <p>
      A common, reasonably effective sequence is two to three years at an agency to build breadth and a
      portfolio across multiple brands, then a move in-house once you know your specialization, to capture
      the higher pay ceiling and the deeper ownership that in-house manager and head-of-marketing roles
      usually offer.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="proof-and-skills">Certifications, tools, and the proof that actually moves you up</h2>
  <p>
    Certifications alone rarely move you up a rung. What moves the needle is a specific, holistic skill mix
    that hiring managers can verify in an interview, not a stack of course-completion certificates.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>The right skill mix for your fork:</strong> Google Analytics/GA4 and a data tool (Excel, Looker Studio) for every fork, plus channel-specific tools: Google Ads and Meta Ads Manager for paid media, Ahrefs or Semrush for SEO, an email/CRM platform (HubSpot, Mailchimp) for content and lifecycle work.</li>
    <li><strong>Proof of work over certificates:</strong> a ranked page you took from zero, a campaign with a documented before/after number, or a content piece tied to a measurable lead is worth more in an interview than any certificate alone. Keep a simple portfolio doc with the number, the context, and what you personally decided.</li>
    <li><strong>Communication:</strong> the ability to explain why a campaign worked or failed in plain language to a non-marketing stakeholder becomes as important as the technical skill itself once you approach manager level.</li>
    <li><strong>Market positioning of yourself:</strong> a specialist known for one specific, defensible outcome ("I grew organic leads 40% in nine months") gets hired and promoted faster than a generalist with a long, unfocused skills list.</li>
    <li><strong>Personal fit:</strong> match the fork to your actual temperament (see the table above), because forcing yourself into a fast-feedback fork like paid media when you are naturally research-driven usually shows up as burnout, not growth.</li>
    <li><strong>Financial reality:</strong> free and low-cost routes (Google Skillshop, HubSpot Academy, Meta Blueprint) cover the fundamentals for every fork; a paid certification or bootcamp is worth it only when it adds mentorship, a real project with feedback, or placement access you cannot get for free, not for the certificate itself.</li>
  </ul>
  <div class="callout callout--teal">
    <p class="callout__eyebrow">Practical read</p>
    <p>
      Pair one hands-on certification with one real project you can talk through in detail. A certificate
      with no story behind it and a strong project with no formal credential are not equal in an interview,
      but a certificate with a genuine project attached beats either one alone.
    </p>
  </div>

  <h2 id="mistakes">What stalls this ladder</h2>
  <div class="protocol-stack">
    {mistakeItems.map((item) => (
      <div class="protocol-row">
        <span class="protocol-num">{item.num}</span>
        <div class="protocol-body">
          <h3>{item.title}</h3>
          <p>{item.body}</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    ))}
  </div>

  <h2 id="related-reads">If you have a different question</h2>
  <p>
    Everything above covers progressing up the employed or agency ladder. If what you actually need sits
    somewhere else, these three cover it in full:
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      Want to skip employment and build income directly with clients instead of climbing a company ladder?
      Read <a href="/blog/freelancing-business/digital-marketing-freelancing-india-how-to-start/">digital
      marketing freelancing India: how to start</a> for the sub-skill packaging, first-client, and pricing
      decisions.
    </li>
    <li>
      Wondering how AI is reshaping what each rung above actually requires? Read
      <a href="/blog/ai-future/ai-changing-digital-marketing-careers-india/">how AI is changing digital
      marketing careers in India</a> for what is automated now and what still pays more because of it.
    </li>
    <li>
      Still deciding between digital marketing and a data-first field entirely, not between digital
      marketing specializations? Read
      <a href="/blog/skills/digital-marketing-vs-data-analytics-career-india/">digital marketing vs data
      analytics career India</a> for the full fit-first comparison.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 id="next-step">What to do next</h2>
  <p>
    If you are at rung one, the most useful thing you can do this month is not another certification, it is
    picking one channel and building one piece of proof you can defend in an interview: a ranked page, a
    campaign you actually managed, or a piece of content tied to a real lead. If you are already a specialist
    wondering why the manager promotion has not come, the honest audit is usually whether you can point to
    real budget or team ownership, not whether you have put in enough years.
  </p>
  <div class="highlight-box highlight-box--teal">
    <p>Pick one channel deliberately by year two or three instead of staying broad by default.</p>
    <p>Build proof of ownership, a budget you managed or a metric you moved, before you chase the next title.</p>
  </div>
  <p>
    Moving toward earlier financial freedom on this ladder comes down to the same thing it always does: the
    right specialization for your working style, visible proof at each rung, and a clear read on whether
    agency breadth or in-house depth fits your next move. If you want a second opinion on where you actually
    sit on this ladder and what the fastest honest next step looks like,
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/career-counselling-and-career-guidance/">career
    guidance</a> can help you map it against your specific background, or start with the free
    <a href="https://futurecareerschool.com/services/assessments">career and skill assessments</a> if you are
    still deciding whether digital marketing is genuinely your lane. You can also explore the wider
    <a href="/career-skills-compass">Career and Skills Compass</a> or browse more
    <a href="/career-resources?topic=skills&type=roadmap">skill roadmaps</a> if a different direction fits
    better.
  </p>

  <h2 id="faq">FAQs on the digital marketing career path in India</h2>
  <div class="faq-accordion">
    {faqItems.map((item, index) => (
      <details open={index === 0}>
        <summary>{item.q}</summary>
        <div class="faq-a">{item.a}</div>
      </details>
    ))}
  </div>

